      "text": "Right. Three months of data, 132 transactions, and the goal is clear — £1,000 more into the emergency fund by July.\n\nHere's what the numbers show.\n\n---\n\nTravel is doing the heavy lifting in your spending — 45% of everything tracked, at £1,388. That's the single biggest lever in the picture. Whether that's a one-off period or a standing pattern is the first question worth answering, because it directly shapes how quickly the fund gets to £2,000.\n\n---\n\nThe direction of travel is good, though. Spending dropped 30% month-over-month — £1,451 this past month versus £2,085 the month before. The biggest single shift was groceries. That kind of move creates real room.\n\n[STATS]\nTracked spend | £3,436\ntravel | 45%\nFood stores | 14\n[/STATS]\n\n---\n\nOn food specifically: across 14 different stores, the average trip comes in at £12, and 19 of those trips were under £5. That's a lot of small, scattered runs rather than consolidated shops. No judgement on the pattern — but it's worth knowing it's there.\n\n---\n\nOne experiment worth trying: move a fixed amount toward the emergency fund on payday, before anything else gets a look at it. The hypothesis is simple — pay yourself first, and the goal fills without requiring willpower later. The success criterion: the agreed amount lands in the fund on payday and stays there for 30 days.\n\n[OPTIONS]\n- Yes, let's try it\n- Pick a different one\n- Break down my travel\n[/OPTIONS]\n\n— C.",
